## Business Recommendations

### 1. Focus on new customers during their first few months

Churn is heavily concentrated among newer customers. The high-risk group had a **median tenure of only 4 months**, while the 0–12 month group had the highest churn rate.

**Recommendation:** Create an early-customer retention program during the first 3–6 months, including onboarding follow-ups, satisfaction checks, and proactive issue resolution.


### 2. Prioritize month-to-month customers

Your analysis consistently showed that **month-to-month customers have much higher churn** than one-year and two-year customers. Also, **100% of your ML high-risk customers were month-to-month**.

**Recommendation:** Offer suitable incentives for month-to-month customers to move to longer contracts, but don't blindly discount everyone. Test targeted offers and measure whether conversion actually reduces churn.



### 3. Investigate the fiber-optic customer experience

**94.7% of your high-risk customers use fiber optic**, and your earlier analysis showed particularly high churn among fiber-optic month-to-month customers.

**Recommendation:** Investigate whether fiber customers are experiencing service quality, pricing, installation, or technical-support issues before assuming fiber itself causes churn.

This distinction matters: **your data shows an association, not proof that fiber causes churn.**



### 4. Strengthen support/security engagement for high-risk customers

Among your high-risk customers:

* **96.7% had no TechSupport**
* **98.1% had no OnlineSecurity**
* **83.7% had no OnlineBackup**

**Recommendation:** Test targeted support/security offers for high-risk customers and monitor whether these interventions reduce churn.


### 5. Use the ML model for proactive retention

Your model achieved:

* **Accuracy:** 80.6%
* **ROC-AUC:** 84.2%
* **High-risk group actual churn:** **71.8%**

**Recommendation:** Use the model to generate a monthly list of high-risk customers so the retention team can contact them **before they leave**, rather than waiting until churn happens.



### 6. Prioritize customers instead of treating everyone equally

A practical workflow could be:

```text
New customer
     ↓
Monitor first 3–6 months
     ↓
Calculate churn probability
     ↓
High risk?
   ↙       ↘
 Yes        No
 ↓           ↓
Retention   Normal
action      monitoring
```

This is much more efficient than giving the same retention campaign to all 7,043 customers.



### 7. Test the recommendations instead of assuming they work

This is the part that will make your project look more like **real analyst work**.

For example:

> Give a retention offer to a selected group of high-risk customers and compare their subsequent churn against a comparable group that did not receive the offer.

Then the company can determine whether the intervention **actually reduces churn**.



